# Chatofy API environment. Copy to .env and fill in the REQUIRED section; the
# rest already works against `docker compose up -d`.
#
# Three sections: REQUIRED (the app does not run without them),
# DEPLOYMENT-SPECIFIC (non-secret, but wrong on any host but a dev laptop) and
# OPTIONAL. Two line forms carry meaning:
#
#   KEY=              unset. The schema reads an empty value as absent, so the
#                     feature behind it stays off.
#   # KEY=value       the default that already lives in
#                     src/config/env.schema.ts. Uncomment ONLY to override — a
#                     copied default is a second place to keep in step, and the
#                     two drifted apart once already.
#
# src/config/env.schema.ts is the authority for types, bounds and defaults, and
# refuses to boot on a value it cannot parse. src/config/env.schema.spec.ts
# holds this file and that schema together.

# ── REQUIRED ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Database. Credentials match docker-compose.yml's defaults (POSTGRES_USER /
# PASSWORD / DB all default to `chatofy`), so this works against
# `docker compose up -d postgres` with no edit. The db-backed e2e suite reads
# the same variable.
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://chatofy:chatofy@localhost:5432/chatofy

# Signs and verifies every access token the API issues. There is no "auth off"
# mode — the app refuses to start without this. Generate one with:
#   openssl rand -base64 32
AUTH_JWT_SECRET=

# Translation is still cloud, so the local speech stack alone does not make
# POST /translate work offline. Absent at BOOT is allowed — a deployment that
# never translates should not be blocked over a key it has no use for — and the
# route then fails with a clear error when it is actually called.
#
# Takes SEVERAL keys, comma-separated. The translator rotates turns across
# them, so one key's free-tier ceiling no longer ends a long live session:
#   GEMINI_API_KEY=key-from-project-a,key-from-project-b,key-from-project-c
#
# READ THIS BEFORE ADDING KEYS — more keys only help under one condition.
# Gemini meters quota per PROJECT per model, not per key (the rejection body
# says so: "GenerateRequestsPerMinutePerProjectPerModel-FreeTier"). Keys issued
# from the SAME Google Cloud project therefore share ONE bucket and rotation
# buys exactly nothing. Each key must come from a different project.
#
# Also note: enabling billing on a project REMOVES that project's free tier, so
# a billed project contributes nothing to a free-tier pool. If you are willing
# to attach a card, one billed project on a paid tier is simpler than several.
#
# Exact duplicates and blank entries are dropped. Same-project keys cannot be
# detected from here — that one is on you.
GEMINI_API_KEY=

# ── DEPLOYMENT-SPECIFIC (non-secret; revisit on every host) ──────────────────
# The defaults below describe a dev laptop. Each one is wrong somewhere else,
# and two of them fail loudly rather than quietly when left alone.

# Origin every mailed link (verify, reset, account-exists notice) is built
# from. The default matches the port apps/web runs on in dev. Production MUST
# override this — the app refuses to boot in production with the default still
# set, since a plausible-looking wrong origin in a mailed link is worse than an
# obviously broken one.
# WEB_BASE_URL=http://localhost:3001

# CORS — comma-separated origins, or * for wildcard. The schema's default is
# `*`; this narrows it to the dev web app, which is why it is a live line.
CORS_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3001

# How many reverse proxies sit in front of this app. 0 = none (direct port).
# REQUIRED to be set correctly behind an ingress or CDN: the auth routes are
# rate limited per client IP, and with this at 0 every request looks like it
# came from the proxy, so one attacker's login attempts exhaust the limit for
# every user. A count, never `true` — `true` makes X-Forwarded-For spoofable
# and removes the limit altogether.
# TRUST_PROXY_HOPS=0

# Holds the rotating refresh-token families — one hash per token and one per
# family, both SHA-256 keyed, never a plaintext credential. The default matches
# docker-compose.yml.
#
# The API BOOTS WITHOUT IT. A stopped Redis costs new sign-ins and token
# renewals, and answers 503 rather than 401 so nobody is signed out over an
# outage. `pnpm --filter api test:e2e` does need one running: the fast e2e
# suites log in for real, and login mints a family.
#
# Still NOT used by the request throttle, whose storage remains this process's
# memory — every per-route limit is per replica. That swap is a separate change.
# REDIS_URL=redis://localhost:6379

# ── OPTIONAL ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# Google login. Every OAuth client id whose id_tokens this API will accept,
# comma-separated. Unset disables the route with a clear error rather than
# blocking the boot — web needs one id, mobile adds a per-platform id later.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_IDS=

# ── Mail (Gmail SMTP) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Read only where NODE_ENV is NEITHER development NOR test. In dev and test the
# console sender always wins and prints links to stdout, whatever is set here —
# so register/verify/forgot/reset work with no Gmail account, and a stray SMTP
# password in a dev .env can never mail a live account-takeover link. To send
# real mail you need NODE_ENV set to something else AND all four values below;
# SMTP_USER wants a Gmail app password (requires 2FA on that account, regular
# passwords are rejected). In such a deployment, missing SMTP means every mail
# is dropped and logged rather than sent, and `NODE_ENV=production` refuses to
# boot at all — see apps/api/src/modules/mail/mail.module.ts and main.ts.
SMTP_HOST=smtp.gmail.com
SMTP_PORT=465
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=

# Optional display-name override, e.g. "Chatofy <SMTP_USER value>". Gmail
# rewrites `From` to the authenticated SMTP_USER address regardless, so an
# address here different from SMTP_USER is silently dropped by Gmail itself.
MAIL_FROM=

# ── Object storage (Cloudflare R2) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# One bucket for the whole project; avatars live under an `avatars/` prefix and
# a later feature adds its own. The bucket is PUBLIC-READ, and a prefix is a
# namespace rather than an access boundary — R2 publishes a bucket as a unit and
# scopes tokens per bucket, not per prefix. Anything that must stay private goes
# in a different bucket. See docs/system-architecture.md.
# OPTIONAL in development, and unset is the normal state. With any of the five
# missing, PUT /auth/me/avatar and DELETE /auth/me/avatar answer 409 with a
# message naming the cause; everything else works normally and the API boots.
#
# 409 rather than 503 deliberately: the shared error contract has no 5xx code but
# INTERNAL_ERROR, and the exception filter replaces every 5xx message — so a 503
# would be indistinguishable from a crash. A 4xx keeps its message.
#
# R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL is the bucket's public custom domain, NOT the
# r2.cloudflarestorage.com endpoint (that is the S3 API and is not publicly
# readable). The web app reads the SAME variable as a build arg to generate its
# CSP img-src, so in production changing the origin is a web rebuild as well as
# an api restart.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
R2_BUCKET=
R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=

# ── Turn-based translate pipeline (vi↔en) ───────────────────────────────────

# Provider selection for the REST POST /translate flow. Speech runs locally by
# default — no API key, no cloud call. Switch either speech provider to
# `elevenlabs` (and set ELEVENLABS_API_KEY) to compare against the cloud path.
# AI_STT_PROVIDER=local
# AI_TTS_PROVIDER=local
# AI_TRANSLATION_PROVIDER=gemini
#
# Any other name selects an OpenAI-compatible host instead — `deepseek` today,
# `openai` if you switch. They exist for the thesis measurement and reach the
# same prompt through the same interface; see benchmarks/error-analysis and
# benchmarks/prompt-injection.
#
# ONE key for the whole family, not one per host, because exactly one
# translation provider is ever selected. A per-host variable would leave every
# deployment carrying blanks for the hosts it did not choose, and would grow
# this file by a line each time the host table grows.
#
# GEMINI_API_KEY above stays separate on purpose: it is not a translation key.
# It also authenticates the realtime and summarization providers, which are
# selected independently of this one.
#
# The key is the ONLY thing about a host that lives here. There is no base URL,
# no model, no reasoning flag and no token-field variable, and that is the
# design. Those three are not choices a deployment makes — they are
# findings about a host, and a host reached with the wrong one keeps translating
# while being slower than the recorded numbers, or fails every turn, with
# nothing in this file to say why. They live beside the host's name in
# src/modules/translate/providers/register-default-providers.ts.
#
# The model is in the table for a reason beyond tidiness: a model id only means
# anything against the endpoint that serves it, so a variable for one without
# the other lets this file express a pair that cannot work. It did — a model
# override naming `gpt-5-nano` while `deepseek` was selected sent that request
# to api.deepseek.com, which answers 400 on every turn. Running a host on a
# second model is a second row in the table, not a variable.
#
# To try a host that is NOT in that table, use the benchmark rather than this
# file — it takes the endpoint, the model and the flags as arguments and needs
# no code change:
#   node benchmarks/error-analysis/translate-rows.mjs rows.jsonl \
#     --provider openai-compatible --base-url https://api.groq.com/openai/v1 \
#     --api-key-env GROQ_API_KEY --extra-body '{"reasoning_effort":"none"}'
OPENAI_COMPATIBLE_API_KEY=

# Only needed when a provider above is set to elevenlabs.
ELEVENLABS_API_KEY=

# Local speech sidecars — start them with `pnpm dev:all` (see their READMEs for
# one-time `uv sync` + model download). Each serves both languages.
#   services/local-stt  — Zipformer (vi) + Parakeet-TDT (en) speech-to-text
#   services/local-tts  — Kokoro-82M (en) + VieNeu (vi) speech synthesis
# Default voices live in the sidecars, since a voice means a speaker id for the
# English engine and a preset name for the Vietnamese one.
# LOCAL_STT_URL=http://localhost:8002
# LOCAL_TTS_URL=http://localhost:8003

# ── Runtime shape ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Both are set by the container images and by the compose files, so a copy of
# this file has no reason to carry them: apps/api/Dockerfile pins the production
# value of NODE_ENV, and docker-compose.prod.yml pins PORT per service.
# NODE_ENV=development
# PORT=3000

# ── Dev/CI tooling (read by Prisma, not by the app) ──────────────────────────
# Used by ONE command: `prisma migrate diff --from-migrations`, which proves the
# migration chain and schema.prisma still agree. Prisma replays the whole chain
# into this database to compare it, so it must be a SEPARATE, disposable one —
# the diff drops and recreates its contents. Never point it at a database
# holding anything.
#
# A deployment runs `migrate deploy`, which needs no shadow database. Leave it
# unset there: it is read only when present, because Prisma rejects an EMPTY
# value outright and that would break every other command.
#
#   docker compose exec postgres psql -U chatofy -d postgres \
#     -c 'CREATE DATABASE chatofy_shadow'
#
# Deliberately absent from src/config/env.schema.ts: nothing in the app reads
# it, so it is allowlisted by name in env.schema.spec.ts.
SHADOW_DATABASE_URL=
